**Statement 1: 100% statement coverage automatically implies 100% branch coverage.**

Statement coverage measures the percentage of statements (e.g., lines of code) executed during a test run. Branch coverage, on the other hand, checks whether all decision points (e.g., if-else statements, switch cases) in the code are executed at least once.

Just because all statements are executed, it doesn't guarantee that all branches are executed. Imagine a scenario where a branch is unreachable due to a previous condition. If that condition is not met, the branch might never be executed, even if all statements are executed.

For example:

```c
